BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rainbow Book Club: Miss Major Speaks by Toshio Meronek & Miss Majo
 r
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260610T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260610T180000
UID:260604GZhb83bE1E-fkZFEb74REA
DESCRIPTION:The Rainbow Book Club is cohosted by Mindy from Napa County Li
 brary and Napa Bookmine staff members Kass and Jules. The book club focuse
 s on books that explore and embrace queer identity. This is a free event\,
  but we appreciate your RSVP so we can plan for your attendance! We look f
 orward to welcoming you. ABOUT THE BOOK 2024 Stonewall Honor Award for Non
 fiction The future of Black\, queer\, and trans liberation explored by a l
 egendary transgender elder and activist. Miss Major Griffin-Gracy is a vet
 eran of the infamous Stonewall Riots\, a former sex worker\, and a transge
 nder elder and activist who has survived Bellevue psychiatric hospital\, A
 ttica Prison\, the HIV/AIDS crisis and a world that white supremacy has bu
 ilt. She has shared tips with other sex workers in the nascent drag ball s
 cene of the late 1960s\, and helped found one of America’s first needle 
 exchange clinics from the back of her van. Miss Major Speaks is both docum
 ent of her brilliant life–told with intimacy\, warmth and an undeniable 
 levity-and a roadmap for the challenges black\, brown\, queer and trans yo
 uth will face on the path to liberation today. Her incredible story of a l
 ife lived and a world survived becomes a conduit for larger questions abou
 t the riddle of collective liberation. For a younger generation\, she warn
 s about the traps of ‘representation\,’ the politics of 'self-care\,' 
 and the frequent dead-ends of non-profit organizing\; for all of us\, she 
 is a strike against those who would erase these histories of struggle. Mis
 s Major offers something that cannot be found elsewhere: an affirmation th
 at our vision for freedom can and must be more expansive than those on off
 er by mainstream institutions. ABOUT THE AUTHOR Miss Major Griffin-Gracy i
 s a living legend in trans/queer circles around the world. She is currentl
 y based in Little Rock\, Arkansas\, where she runs the House of GG educati
 onal retreat and historical center. Toshio Meronek is a San Francisco-base
 d writer focusing on housing and queer politics
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/916728548067897/?acontext=%7B%22event_
 action_history%22%3A[%7B%22mechanism%22%3A%22lov_feed%22%2C%22surface%22%3
 A%22bookmark%22%7D]%2C%22ref_notif_type%22%3Anull%7D
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
